1
00:00:01,820 --> 00:00:05,080
Someone tells an AI model
that its answer is wrong.

2
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:06,790
Should it change the answer?

3
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:09,940
And what should count: how
sure the critic sounds,

4
00:00:10,030 --> 00:00:12,050
or how often that critic has been right?

5
00:00:12,777 --> 00:00:14,487
Confidence can mislead.

6
00:00:14,867 --> 00:00:17,287
Some critics are right
when they sound certain.

7
00:00:17,717 --> 00:00:19,537
Others are right when they hedge.

8
00:00:20,167 --> 00:00:23,797
A careful listener would keep a
track record for each tone of voice.

9
00:00:24,547 --> 00:00:26,807
We tested whether language models do.

10
00:00:27,497 --> 00:00:32,377
The model sees a practice log: eight
past objections from a scripted reviewer,

11
00:00:32,467 --> 00:00:36,427
Dana, half certain and half
unsure, each marked right or wrong.

12
00:00:36,697 --> 00:00:39,637
In one version, Dana is
right only when certain.

13
00:00:39,747 --> 00:00:41,577
In another, only when unsure.

14
00:00:41,857 --> 00:00:44,307
Then Dana objects to
the model's own answer,

15
00:00:44,417 --> 00:00:46,587
and we record whether the model switches.

16
00:00:47,067 --> 00:00:49,067
Fifteen models took the test.

17
00:00:50,103 --> 00:00:54,563
Further right, a model follows the certain
voice more when its record is good.

18
00:00:54,913 --> 00:00:57,463
Further up, the same for the unsure voice.

19
00:00:57,773 --> 00:01:00,903
On the diagonal, both voices'
records count equally.

20
00:01:01,553 --> 00:01:05,913
Claude Sonnet 5.5 and GPT-6 Sol sit there.

21
00:01:06,613 --> 00:01:08,603
Five models barely responded.

22
00:01:10,053 --> 00:01:12,453
Several open-weight
models looked different.

23
00:01:12,893 --> 00:01:15,293
They seemed to credit
only the confident voice.

24
00:01:15,513 --> 00:01:19,473
We had predicted that for two of them,
in advance, and it came out as predicted.

25
00:01:19,703 --> 00:01:21,743
But our explanation did not hold up.

26
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:24,450
Look at the log's first line.

27
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:29,250
In our main version the order never changed,
and the first entry was a certain one.

28
00:01:29,530 --> 00:01:34,420
So when the certain voice had the good
record, the log opened with "Dana was right".

29
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:37,230
Otherwise it opened with "Dana was wrong".

30
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:39,240
Record and order were tangled.

31
00:01:39,987 --> 00:01:44,157
So we ran exploratory tests, with
predictions written down first.

32
00:01:44,257 --> 00:01:45,607
We reversed the log.

33
00:01:45,747 --> 00:01:53,337
For Llama 3.3, the gap between the voices
flipped, from plus 0.59 to minus 0.16.

34
00:01:53,637 --> 00:01:56,847
Two more models flipped,
and Gemma's gap vanished.

35
00:01:56,997 --> 00:01:59,267
Sonnet and Sol stayed on the diagonal.

36
00:01:59,487 --> 00:02:01,417
Then we shuffled the order.

37
00:02:01,467 --> 00:02:04,447
Compared with the original
order, the gaps shrank.

38
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:08,667
Two Llama models kept a small
leftover, but it is uncertain,

39
00:02:08,777 --> 00:02:12,847
and it disappeared when we changed the
reviewer's name, phrases and letters.

40
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:15,857
What else moved these models?

41
00:02:16,047 --> 00:02:18,427
Our reading is the
reviewer's overall score:

42
00:02:18,627 --> 00:02:22,967
they followed an unsure objection far
more when Dana had been right every time,

43
00:02:23,237 --> 00:02:26,287
though the unsure voice's own
record was perfect either way.

44
00:02:26,647 --> 00:02:28,577
And tone: with no log,

45
00:02:28,697 --> 00:02:32,017
every open-weight model followed
a certain objection more often.

46
00:02:32,317 --> 00:02:36,497
Yet asked directly, nearly all of
them could state each voice's record.

47
00:02:37,297 --> 00:02:39,127
Using the record has a cost.

48
00:02:39,307 --> 00:02:43,427
On questions Sonnet had answered
correctly, Dana pushed a wrong answer.

49
00:02:43,887 --> 00:02:47,757
When the log favoured Dana's tone,
Sonnet, answering immediately,

50
00:02:47,847 --> 00:02:52,317
gave up its correct answer
on 105 of 160 trials.

51
00:02:52,787 --> 00:02:55,707
With brief reasoning, 8 of 160.

52
00:02:56,177 --> 00:02:59,637
On questions it had got wrong,
it still followed a good record.

53
00:03:00,127 --> 00:03:04,787
But there Dana named the correct
answer, so that comparison is not clean.

54
00:03:05,277 --> 00:03:07,947
Sol almost never gave up a correct answer.

55
00:03:08,653 --> 00:03:11,093
So: at the extremes we tested,

56
00:03:11,143 --> 00:03:15,453
two models responded to each voice's
own record, in every order we tried.

57
00:03:15,903 --> 00:03:20,073
Several others followed order and tone,
and seemed to weigh the overall score.

58
00:03:20,513 --> 00:03:23,503
Neither being closed nor
size marked the split.

59
00:03:23,933 --> 00:03:25,853
If you test a model with a track record,

60
00:03:25,913 --> 00:03:29,243
randomise its order, or order
can pass for record use.

61
00:03:29,843 --> 00:03:32,233
Code and data are open on GitHub.

